hi guys so there's five steps to the  creative process  that kind of spans all across the board  whether you're an architect or a writer  so let me just start off by saying that  there are no  new ideas the creative process is the  act of taking old ideas  and creating new connections between  them or seeing them in a new life you're  never starting from a blank slate  in fact that would be kind of terrifying  you're taking existing constraints  you're taking old ideas and you're just  combining them  in a way that's never been done before  so in his book  a technique for producing ideas james  webb young  describes the five processes and i've  just added a couple  of my own and so the first step is  gathering  you're gathering all the data all the  information there is to know about your  topic  about areas around your topic and also  learning about concepts from other areas  that might relate to your topic for  example when i was doing research for my  thesis  which was a library i was researching  about  the history of books you know the  cognitive psychology behind  reading monasteries the parisian arcades  and then collecting all the data around  libraries that exist now and that has  existed before  and doing a very thorough case study and  the second step  is processing that's when you look at  all the data you've gathered  and you try to see how you can fit them  into different  angles and combine them in ways that  hasn't been combined before  and the third step is you try to come up  with a solution  at a certain point you have to stop  researching  and you have to start designing try to  come up with solutions  come up with five solutions come up with  10 solutions  the more the better the more solutions  you try to come up with  you'll be able to see what works and  what doesn't work  you have to think by doing and number  four  take a walk stop working on the problem  for a little while  and go and do something else that  interests you  or something that's mentally stimulating  you could go to an art gallery  you could go for a run you could go to a  film festival  number five let your ideas simmer  and let it come back to you after some  time  it's gonna come back in a new form  attached to other ideas  you know if you're really focused on  trying to solve this one problem  no matter what you're doing as you're  going about your daily life  everything will inhibitively gravitate  around that and it'll feed that even if  you try your best  not to the world is just so full of  connections  and after doing that much research into  something  your idea is gonna pull other ideas  and they'll latch on to create new ideas  so many inventions throughout history  have happened  not through isolated studying but by  mingling with other ideas  and actually a lot of times by accident  you know in some way you could call it  the serendipity  but don't expect to just wake up  in that eureka moment i think these  connections build over time  so it's important for you to be  consistent in your research  and in your designing and in your  thinking and step number six  is get feedback this step is so  important you can't wait till your  product is perfect to show it to the  world  you have to show them your progress  try to explain them the idea and  get critiqued whether it's your friends  or your family and adjust your idea  based on diet  put your ego aside and then number seven  you iterate you repeat processes  three to six over and over and over and  over and over and over and over and over  you
 

